Hi All,
I just want to expand upon the question to see if this makes it easier to answer (apologizing in advance for my rudimentary knowledge of R programming). I want to create a matched data file (csv or sas) so that the unit of observation/analysis is the matched pair. I've been studying syntax examples in the manual and from the forum. When I try to use the syntax that Mike Foster posted in 2008, I get matched pairs that are seemingly identified with a system assigned observation number rather than my ID variable. How do I use the row.names command to specify my ID variable as the observation name (See example on page 36 but I have 24,000 observations in my data set) in match.matrix? Here is the syntax that Mike posted in 2008. I don't understand what "mm" is and how to make this syntax work for me --or, rather, it's working but I can't seem to successfully assign my ID variable as the observation name.
   pscore<-read.dta("working.dta")

    m.out<- matchit(Y~<xvar> , model="optimal" , data=pscore)
    m.data<-match.data(m.out)
    summary(m.out)mm

    mm<-m.out$match.matrix
    tx<-as.numeric(row.names(mm))
    m<-cbind(tx,as.numeric(mm))
    m[1:5,]

    write.dta(data.frame(m),"mmatrix.dta")

I'll be tremendously appreciative of any advice or syntax examples that anyone could offer.
Best,
Sharon


On Tue, Oct 22, 2013 at 6:03 PM, Sharon Simonton <simonton-63aXycvo3TyHXe+LvDLADg at public.gmane.org<mailto:simonton-63aXycvo3TyHXe+LvDLADg at public.gmane.org>> wrote:
Hello,
I've been trying to print out (or export) a data set containing the IDs for my matched pairs to read them into SAS/Mplus.  I just realized that what I've been doing is not working correctly.
I've been using the syntax example in the manual given for seeing the outcomes for matched pairs to try to print out the IDs for my matched pairs.
cbind (dataset [row.names (m.matchset$match.matrix), "I_ID"], dataset [m.matchset$match.matrix, "I_ID])
This doesn't seem to be giving me what I want. How can I get IDs and a unique pair number for my matched pairs so that I can do conditional logistic regression?
Thank you,
Sharon Simonton

